<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ka'ba-ye Zartosht</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kaaba of Zoroaster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ka'ba-ye Zartosht is a square stone tower of the Achaemenid period at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>; its original purpose is still debated. Its walls carry the long trilingual inscription of the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a>, Parthian and Greek, recording his victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the lands of his realm, as well as an inscription of the high priest Kartir.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaba-ye-zartosht.jpg" alt="Ka'ba-ye Zartosht" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naghsh-e_rostam,_Ir%C3%A1n,_2016-09-24,_DD_14.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kings were usually clients of a stronger power: of Rome after Pompey's campaign, and from the third century of the Sasanian Empire, whose king Shapur I listed Albania among the lands of his realm in his great inscription on the <b>Ka'ba-ye Zartosht</b> at Naqsh-e Rustam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Persians called it Parsig, “Persian”; under the Sasanians it was also called Ērānīg, “belonging to Iran”, and the high priest Kartir’s inscription on the <b>Ka'ba-ye Zartosht</b> speaks of “ērānīg ud anērānīg”, the language of the Iranians and the non-Iranians.</blockquote><a href="/language/middle-persian">Middle Persian · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
